WEBVTT

0
00:00.200 --> 00:01.300
<v Krista>In this video, we're talking about</v>

1
00:01.300 --> 00:03.470
how to write numbers in scientific notation.

2
00:03.470 --> 00:05.420
And you have to remember that scientific notation

3
00:05.420 --> 00:07.930
is just a different way of writing the same number.

4
00:07.930 --> 00:10.100
The reason we have to learn about scientific notation

5
00:10.100 --> 00:12.410
is because it can be really useful to write numbers

6
00:12.410 --> 00:15.200
in scientific notation when the numbers are really big

7
00:15.200 --> 00:17.060
and have a lot of zeros at the end

8
00:17.060 --> 00:18.730
or really, really small decimals

9
00:18.730 --> 00:20.530
and have a lot of zeros in the decimal.

10
00:20.530 --> 00:23.270
Scientific notation is kind of a way of writing the number

11
00:23.270 --> 00:25.640
that allows you to collapse all of the zeros

12
00:25.640 --> 00:28.370
and make it easier to look at the value of the number.

13
00:28.370 --> 00:30.520
When you're writing something in scientific notation,

14
00:30.520 --> 00:32.770
you're always going to need a whole number and a decimal.

15
00:32.770 --> 00:35.210
So for example, if I have a whole number five

16
00:35.210 --> 00:38.900
and a decimal point three, I'm going to have 5.3.

17
00:38.900 --> 00:41.790
And then we say times, 10 to some number

18
00:41.790 --> 00:43.850
and it can be a positive or a negative number.

19
00:43.850 --> 00:45.710
So for example, 10 to the fourth.

20
00:45.710 --> 00:46.930
The only reason I'm showing you this

21
00:46.930 --> 00:48.520
is because this is just an example

22
00:48.520 --> 00:51.030
of standard form of scientific notation.

23
00:51.030 --> 00:52.160
It's just important to remember

24
00:52.160 --> 00:53.840
that you're always going to have this whole number,

25
00:53.840 --> 00:55.440
you're always going to have a decimal here

26
00:55.440 --> 00:57.900
and then you're going to have times 10 to,

27
00:57.900 --> 00:59.790
either a positive or a negative exponent.

28
00:59.790 --> 01:01.200
So that's sort of standard form

29
01:01.200 --> 01:03.480
and we want to take all of these numbers we have here

30
01:03.480 --> 01:05.050
and write them in this standard form.

31
01:05.050 --> 01:06.150
So the first thing you want to do

32
01:06.150 --> 01:07.450
when you're given a number you need to write

33
01:07.450 --> 01:10.130
in scientific notation is locate the decimal point.

34
01:10.130 --> 01:12.690
In this first example, we've been given the number 2,400.

35
01:12.690 --> 01:15.770
So the decimal point is at the very end of the number.

36
01:15.770 --> 01:18.900
This is the same as 2400.0 for example.

37
01:18.900 --> 01:20.820
But the decimal place is right here.

38
01:20.820 --> 01:22.900
Because standard form for scientific notation

39
01:22.900 --> 01:24.730
tells us that we're going to need this whole number

40
01:24.730 --> 01:26.200
and we're going to need this decimal,

41
01:26.200 --> 01:28.680
we can see here that we have two and four.

42
01:28.680 --> 01:32.190
So if we move the decimal point over three places,

43
01:32.190 --> 01:35.250
one, two, three places like this,

44
01:35.250 --> 01:36.550
it's going to end up right here

45
01:36.550 --> 01:38.450
and we'll have two as our whole number

46
01:38.450 --> 01:39.920
and four as our decimal.

47
01:39.920 --> 01:40.753
So that's perfect.

48
01:40.753 --> 01:43.840
So we'll go ahead and say 2.4 times,

49
01:43.840 --> 01:46.650
and now the number of places that we moved the decimal

50
01:46.650 --> 01:49.720
is going to dictate what our exponent is here.

51
01:49.720 --> 01:52.340
So we get times 10 to the three.

52
01:52.340 --> 01:53.390
So we know we're going to have three,

53
01:53.390 --> 01:55.580
because we moved the decimal point three places.

54
01:55.580 --> 01:57.030
Now the only question is,

55
01:57.030 --> 01:59.540
do we say positive three or negative three.

56
01:59.540 --> 02:01.930
Well, when you move the decimal place to the left,

57
02:01.930 --> 02:03.470
you're going to have a positive exponent.

58
02:03.470 --> 02:05.340
When you move the decimal place to the right,

59
02:05.340 --> 02:06.920
you're going to have a negative exponent.

60
02:06.920 --> 02:08.310
And you can always double check yourself

61
02:08.310 --> 02:09.840
by multiplying this back out.

62
02:09.840 --> 02:12.760
Because really this is just 2.4 times,

63
02:12.760 --> 02:15.750
10 to the three is 10 times 10, which is 100,

64
02:15.750 --> 02:17.540
times 10 again which is 1,000.

65
02:17.540 --> 02:21.020
So we get times 1,000 and 2.4 times 1,000,

66
02:21.020 --> 02:23.430
if you do that multiplication, is 2,400.

67
02:23.430 --> 02:25.600
So you can always prove it to yourself if you're not sure

68
02:25.600 --> 02:27.960
that your scientific notation was done correctly.

69
02:27.960 --> 02:29.678
So again, if we move the decimal place to the left,

70
02:29.678 --> 02:31.340
it's a positive exponent,

71
02:31.340 --> 02:33.440
if we move it to the right, it's a negative exponent.

72
02:33.440 --> 02:34.900
Let's look at another example here.

73
02:34.900 --> 02:36.920
We have 720,000.

74
02:36.920 --> 02:38.940
Well again, just like with this first example,

75
02:38.940 --> 02:41.100
the decimal place is going to be here at the end.

76
02:41.100 --> 02:42.457
We're going to have a whole number of seven

77
02:42.457 --> 02:44.180
and a decimal place of two

78
02:44.180 --> 02:47.370
if we move the decimal place over, one, two,

79
02:47.370 --> 02:50.110
three, four, five places.

80
02:50.110 --> 02:54.880
So then this is going to become, 7.2 times 10 to the five.

81
02:54.880 --> 02:55.990
We know it's going to be 10 to the five

82
02:55.990 --> 02:57.310
cause we moved it five places

83
02:57.310 --> 02:59.750
and because we moved the decimal place to the left,

84
02:59.750 --> 03:01.940
we know that our exponent is going to be positive.

85
03:01.940 --> 03:03.100
Let's look at a couple of examples

86
03:03.100 --> 03:04.460
where we need to move the decimal place

87
03:04.460 --> 03:06.090
to the right instead of to the left.

88
03:06.090 --> 03:11.090
If we locate our decimal right here, we have 0.00016.

89
03:11.770 --> 03:13.900
If we move the decimal place

90
03:13.900 --> 03:17.540
one, two, three, four places to the right,

91
03:17.540 --> 03:18.750
one will be our whole number

92
03:18.750 --> 03:20.460
and six will be our decimal place

93
03:20.460 --> 03:24.510
so we'll get 1.6 when the decimal ends up right here.

94
03:24.510 --> 03:26.770
We'll get 1.6 and we moved the decimal over

95
03:26.770 --> 03:28.690
one, two, three, four places.

96
03:28.690 --> 03:31.410
So we're going to have times 10 to the fourth.

97
03:31.410 --> 03:33.610
But because we moved the decimal to the right,

98
03:33.610 --> 03:36.060
this has to be 10 to the negative four.

99
03:36.060 --> 03:37.525
If you want to double check yourself here,

100
03:37.525 --> 03:40.140
what we need to remember is this rule of exponents

101
03:40.140 --> 03:43.050
where if you have a negative exponent in the numerator here,

102
03:43.050 --> 03:44.930
it's the same thing as a positive exponent

103
03:44.930 --> 03:45.763
in the denominator.

104
03:45.763 --> 03:47.921
So 1.6 times 10 to the negative four,

105
03:47.921 --> 03:51.700
is actually the same thing is 1.6 times

106
03:51.700 --> 03:54.210
one over 10 to the positive four,

107
03:54.210 --> 03:55.830
instead of ten to the negative four.

108
03:55.830 --> 04:00.830
Then you'd have 1.6 over 10 to the fourth, which is 10,000.

109
04:01.480 --> 04:05.160
And then if you do 1.6 divided by 10,000 on your calculator

110
04:05.160 --> 04:06.860
what you'll see is that we get back

111
04:06.860 --> 04:08.963
to our original answer, 0.00016.

112
04:11.160 --> 04:15.680
Now what about with this last example here, we have 0.05.

113
04:15.680 --> 04:19.470
Well we only have one non zero number here of five.

114
04:19.470 --> 04:20.640
So you might be wondering,

115
04:20.640 --> 04:22.120
I can make my whole number five,

116
04:22.120 --> 04:23.430
but what's going to be my decimal.

117
04:23.430 --> 04:24.920
Well all we really have to do here

118
04:24.920 --> 04:26.820
is add an extra zero on the end,

119
04:26.820 --> 04:28.390
we haven't changed anything, right?

120
04:28.390 --> 04:31.630
0.05 is the same as 0.050.

121
04:31.630 --> 04:34.360
And now we just want to move our decimal to the right,

122
04:34.360 --> 04:37.100
one, two places, so that it's here.

123
04:37.100 --> 04:40.830
And then in scientific notation we'll just call this 5.0.

124
04:40.830 --> 04:42.440
I'm doing this example to illustrate

125
04:42.440 --> 04:44.900
that zero can be the decimal place.

126
04:44.900 --> 04:46.070
You don't always have to have

127
04:46.070 --> 04:48.320
a non zero number in this decimal spot here.

128
04:48.320 --> 04:51.220
It can be 5.0 or anything point zero.

129
04:51.220 --> 04:54.710
So we're going to get 5.0 times 10 to the two,

130
04:54.710 --> 04:57.560
because we moved to the decimal one, two places.

131
04:57.560 --> 04:59.490
But it's going to have to be negative two,

132
04:59.490 --> 05:01.850
because we moved the decimal to the right.

133
05:01.850 --> 05:03.382
And that's how you use scientific notation

134
05:03.382 --> 05:06.010
to sort of collapse all of these zeros

135
05:06.010 --> 05:08.420
when you have very large numbers like these two

136
05:08.420 --> 05:10.593
or very small numbers like these ones.